  "case": {
    "presentation": "Patient is a 28 year old man who has urinary frequency despite treatment for a presumed UTI. Patient has nocturia and workup for polyuria was done. CMP and A1c were normal but urine sodium and urine osm were both low. What further workup should be done to work up diabetes insipidus or other etiologies?",
    "recommendations": "The patient's low urine osm can be seen in primary polydipsia, low solute intake or diabetes insipidus. Would recommend checking a 24h urine (urine volume, creatinine and osmolality) to determine whether the patient has polyuria. If patient is confirmed to have polyuria (>3L/day), then would refer to endocrine for DI evaluation. Would also assess the patient's water and solute intake to evaluate for primary polydipsia, as well as whether UTI was adequately treated (ongoing dysuria leading to increased water intake) through history and repeat UA."
  },
